    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="po-what">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="split split--top">
          <div>
            <p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Overview</p>
            <h2 class="title" id="po-what" data-fx="wipe">What is <span class="grad-text">pediatric oncology?</span></h2>
            <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Pediatric oncology is the branch of medicine that diagnoses and treats cancer in babies, children and teenagers, usually from birth up to 18 years of age. A pediatric hemato-oncologist treats both childhood blood cancers and blood disorders.</p>
            <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">With the right pediatric oncology treatment in Bangalore, children can finish treatment and go back to school, play and a normal life. Dr. Neema Bhat, a US board-certified pediatrician and pediatric hematologist-oncologist with 10+ years of experience, treats childhood blood cancers, solid tumours and blood disorders, including bone marrow transplant.</p>
            <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">About 3 out of every 100 cancer patients in India are children under 15, and leukemia makes up about 40% of childhood cancers (Indian Pediatrics study). In high-income countries, more than 80% of children with cancer survive (WHO). Early diagnosis and expert care help Indian children get the same chance.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-child"/></svg>Childhood cancers are different</h3><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>They usually start in growing cells, such as the blood, brain, kidneys, nerves, bones and muscles.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>They are rarely linked to lifestyle, such as diet or smoking.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>They often grow fast, but they also usually respond well to treatment.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Treatment doses and medicines are planned for a child’s age, weight and growing body.</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">Children are not small adults. They need a team trained to care for their bodies, their development and their emotions, along with support for the whole family.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="po-sym">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Warning signs</p><h2 class="title" id="po-sym" data-fx="wipe">Warning signs of cancer <span class="grad-text">in children</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Most of these signs are caused by common, harmless illnesses. But if a sign lasts more than two weeks, keeps coming back, or gets worse, see a doctor. Watch for:</p>
        </div><ul class="sym sym--3"><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-activity"/></svg><b>Paleness &amp; tiredness</b><span>Paleness, tiredness or weakness that does not go away</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg><b>Easy bruising</b><span>Small red spots on the skin, or unusual bleeding (nose, gums)</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-thermo"/></svg><b>Repeated fever</b><span>Fever or infections that keep coming back without a clear cause</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-cells"/></svg><b>A lump or swelling</b><span>In the tummy, neck, armpit, groin or any part of the body</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-cells"/></svg><b>Swollen glands</b><span>That do not shrink after a few weeks</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-bone"/></svg><b>Bone or joint pain</b><span>Limping, or pain that wakes the child at night</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-brain"/></svg><b>Morning headaches</b><span>Headaches, especially in the morning, with vomiting</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-adult"/></svg><b>Changes in walking</b><span>Balance, vision or behaviour, or new fits (seizures)</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-eye"/></svg><b>Eye changes</b><span>A white glow in the pupil in photos, a new squint, or eye swelling</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-scale"/></svg><b>Weight loss</b><span>Loss of appetite or night sweats</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg><b>Swollen tummy</b><span>A swollen or hard tummy</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-users"/></svg><b>In teenagers</b><span>A painless lump in the testicle</span></li></ul><p class="alert" data-fx="fade"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg><span><b>See a doctor urgently</b> if your child has a high fever with very pale skin or bleeding, breathing difficulty, severe headache with vomiting, a fit, sudden weakness of the legs, or is unusually drowsy.</span></p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="po-cause">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="cols2">
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3 id="po-cause"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-flask"/></svg>What causes cancer in children?</h3><p class="box__sub">In most children, the cause of cancer is not known. Childhood cancer usually starts from random changes (mutations) in the genes of growing cells. It is not caused by anything the parents did or did not do, and it is not contagious. A small number of children have a higher risk because of:</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Inherited genetic conditions:</b> such as Down syndrome (higher risk of leukemia), Li-Fraumeni syndrome, neurofibromatosis and hereditary retinoblastoma</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>A family history of retinoblastoma or certain inherited cancer syndromes</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Some immune system disorders that are present from birth</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Previous cancer treatment:</b> chemotherapy or radiation given for an earlier cancer</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Certain infections:</b> such as Epstein-Barr virus (EBV), linked to some lymphomas, and HIV</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">Because most childhood cancers cannot be prevented, early diagnosis is the most important step for a good outcome.</p></div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.08s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-clock"/></svg>Why early treatment matters</h3><p class="box__sub">Childhood cancers often grow quickly, so a delay of even a few weeks can change the outcome. If cancer in a child is not treated early:</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>The cancer can spread to other organs, making it harder to treat.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Serious complications can develop, such as severe infections, bleeding, kidney problems or pressure on the brain or spinal cord.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>More intensive treatment may be needed, which can increase the risk of long-term side effects.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>The chance of cure drops, especially for leukemia, retinoblastoma and solid tumours.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Vision, limbs or organs may be lost in cancers like retinoblastoma and bone tumours that could otherwise be saved.</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">The good news: when found early and treated at a specialised centre, most childhood cancers can be controlled, and many can be cured.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="po-types">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head head--center">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Conditions</p><h2 class="title" id="po-types" data-fx="wipe">Childhood cancers and blood disorders <span class="grad-text">we treat</span></h2></div>
          
        </div><div class="dtable-wrap" data-fx="fade"><table class="dtable"><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>What it means in simple words</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label="Condition"><b><a href="/leukemia-treatment">Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL)</a></b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">The most common childhood cancer. Abnormal white cells fill the bone marrow. Most children are cured with treatment.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Condition"><b>Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) and chronic myeloid leukemia (CML)</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Other blood cancers that start in the bone marrow.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Condition"><b>Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin <a href="/lymphoma-treatment">lymphoma</a></b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Cancers of the lymph nodes and immune system, often showing as painless swollen glands.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Condition"><b><a href="/brain-tumor-treatment">Brain and spinal cord tumours</a></b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Growths in the brain or spine that can cause headaches, vomiting, or problems with walking or vision.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Condition"><b>Neuroblastoma</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">A tumour of nerve cells, often in the tummy, mostly in children under 5.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Condition"><b>Wilms tumour (nephroblastoma)</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">A kidney tumour in young children, often found as a swelling in the tummy.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Condition"><b>Retinoblastoma</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">An eye cancer in babies and young children, often seen as a white glow in the pupil.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Condition"><b>Bone tumours (osteosarcoma, Ewing sarcoma)</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Cancers of the bone, usually in older children and teenagers, causing pain or swelling.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Condition"><b>Rhabdomyosarcoma and other soft tissue sarcomas</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Cancers of the muscles and soft tissues.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Condition"><b>Hepatoblastoma</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">A liver tumour in young children.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Condition"><b>Germ cell tumours</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Tumours of the ovaries, testes or other parts of the body.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Condition"><b>Histiocytic disorders (LCH, HLH)</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words">Rare conditions where certain immune cells grow or act abnormally.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Condition"><b>Childhood blood disorders</b></td><td data-label="What it means in simple words"><a href="/thalassemia-treatment">Thalassemia</a>, sickle cell disease, aplastic anemia, ITP, hemophilia and severe <a href="/anemia-treatment">anemia</a>.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="po-dx">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Diagnosis</p><h2 class="title" id="po-dx" data-fx="wipe">How is childhood cancer <span class="grad-text">diagnosed?</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Dr. Neema Bhat chooses tests based on your child’s symptoms and age. Painful tests are done under sedation or anaesthesia so that your child is comfortable. Tests may include:</p>
        </div><ol class="olist"><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">01</span><div><b>Physical examination and history</b><p>Including growth, development and family history.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">02</span><div><b>CBC and peripheral smear</b><p>Check blood counts and look for abnormal cells.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">03</span><div><b>Blood chemistry tests</b><p>Kidney and liver function, LDH, uric acid and electrolytes.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">04</span><div><b>Tumour markers</b><p>Such as AFP, beta-hCG and urine catecholamines for certain tumours.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">05</span><div><b>Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy</b><p>To diagnose leukemia and check if other cancers have spread to the marrow.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">06</span><div><b>Flow cytometry</b><p>Identifies the exact type of leukemia or lymphoma.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">07</span><div><b>Cytogenetics, FISH &amp; molecular tests</b><p>Find genetic changes that guide treatment and predict risk.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">08</span><div><b>Imaging</b><p>Ultrasound, X-ray, CT, MRI or PET-CT to find the tumour and check its spread.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">09</span><div><b>Biopsy</b><p>A small sample of the tumour or lymph node is examined to confirm the diagnosis.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">10</span><div><b>Lumbar puncture (spinal tap)</b><p>Checks whether cancer cells are in the fluid around the brain and spine.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">11</span><div><b>Eye examination under anaesthesia</b><p>For suspected retinoblastoma.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">12</span><div><b>Heart, hearing and kidney tests</b><p>Before treatment, as a baseline.</p></div></li></ol>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="po-tx">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Treatment</p><h2 class="title" id="po-tx" data-fx="wipe">Pediatric oncology treatment <span class="grad-text">options</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Treatment depends on the type of cancer, its stage and risk group, the child’s age and overall health. Most children receive a combination of treatments, planned by a team of specialists.</p>
        </div><div class="cards3"><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-syringe"/></svg><h3>Risk-adapted chemotherapy</h3><p>Anti-cancer medicines given in planned cycles, with doses based on the child’s risk group, weight and age. Many cycles can be given as daycare treatment.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-pill"/></svg><h3>Targeted therapy</h3><p>Medicines that attack specific changes in cancer cells, such as in some leukemias.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg><h3>Immunotherapy</h3><p>Monoclonal antibodies and other treatments that help the immune system fight cancer cells.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-cells"/></svg><h3>CAR-T cell therapy</h3><p>For selected children with relapsed leukemia or lymphoma, where suitable and available.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-stetho"/></svg><h3>Surgery</h3><p>To remove solid tumours such as Wilms tumour, neuroblastoma, liver and bone tumours, including limb-sparing surgery for bone cancers.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-radiation"/></svg><h3>Radiation therapy</h3><p>Carefully planned radiation for certain tumours, using techniques that protect healthy growing tissue.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-marrow"/></svg><h3>Bone marrow (stem cell) transplant</h3><p>Autologous or donor transplant for high-risk or relapsed leukemia, neuroblastoma and other cancers, and for blood disorders like thalassemia and aplastic anemia. <a href="/bone-marrow-transplant">Learn more</a></p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-link"/></svg><h3>Central line or port</h3><p>A long-term line to give medicines and take blood without repeated needle pricks.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg><h3>Supportive care</h3><p>Transfusions, prevention and treatment of infections, anti-sickness medicines and fertility preservation counselling for teenagers.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg><h3>Pain management &amp; palliative care</h3><p>To keep the child comfortable at every stage of treatment.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-apple"/></svg><h3>Nutrition support</h3><p>A diet plan to maintain weight and strength during treatment.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-chat"/></svg><h3>Psychological and child-life support</h3><p>Counselling and play therapy for the child, brothers and sisters, and parents.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-clock"/></svg><h3>Long-term follow-up</h3><p>Survivorship care: regular checks for growth, learning, heart, hormone and fertility effects after treatment ends.</p></div></div><p class="alert alert--warn" data-fx="fade"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg><span><b>Never stop or change treatment on your own, even if your child looks well.</b> Completing the full treatment plan gives the best chance of cure.</span></p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="po-care">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3 id="po-care"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-users"/></svg>Caring for your child during and after treatment</h3><p class="box__sub">Parents play a big part in their child’s recovery. These steps help:</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Prevent infections:</b> wash hands often, keep the child away from people with fever, cough or chickenpox, and keep the central line or port clean and dry.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Check for fever:</b> a temperature of 100.4°F (38°C) or more during treatment is an emergency. Go to the hospital straight away.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Give safe food:</b> freshly cooked, hot home food and boiled or filtered water. Avoid street food, raw salads and cut fruit kept outside.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Encourage eating and drinking:</b> small, frequent meals help when appetite is low.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Look after the mouth:</b> gentle brushing with a soft brush and regular mouthwash as advised.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Follow the vaccine schedule your oncologist gives. Live vaccines are usually avoided during treatment.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Keep life as normal as possible:</b> play, schooling (at home or in school, as allowed) and time with family support your child’s emotional health.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Look after yourself too:</b> talk to the care team, counsellors and other parents for support.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Keep every follow-up visit after treatment ends. Survivorship care checks growth, learning, heart, hormones and fertility for years.</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">Most children who finish treatment return to school and grow up to lead healthy, active lives.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

