        <div class="faq2__list"><details class="qa" open><summary><span class="qa__n">01</span><span class="qa__q">Who is the right doctor for cancer in children in Bangalore?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">A pediatric oncologist or pediatric hemato-oncologist is the right specialist. Dr. Neema Bhat is a US board-certified pediatric hematologist-oncologist in Bangalore who treats childhood cancers and blood disorders.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">02</span><span class="qa__q">What is the most common cancer in children?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Leukemia, especially acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), is the most common childhood cancer. In India, lymphomas and brain tumours are the next most common.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">03</span><span class="qa__q">Can childhood cancer be cured?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Yes, many childhood cancers can be cured. In high-income countries, more than 80% of children with cancer survive. Results are best when treatment starts early at a specialised centre and is completed fully.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">04</span><span class="qa__q">What are the early signs of cancer in children?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Common signs are paleness, easy bruising, repeated fever, a lump or swelling, bone pain, morning headaches with vomiting, and a white glow in the eye. See a doctor if any sign lasts more than two weeks.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">05</span><span class="qa__q">Did I cause my child’s cancer?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">No. Most childhood cancers happen because of random gene changes in growing cells. They are not caused by anything parents did or did not do.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">06</span><span class="qa__q">How long does childhood cancer treatment take?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">It depends on the cancer. Some solid tumours are treated in 6 to 12 months. Treatment for ALL usually takes about 2 to 3 years, though most of it is given as outpatient care.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">07</span><span class="qa__q">Will my child need to stay in hospital throughout treatment?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">No. Children stay in hospital for some intensive phases, but much of the treatment is given as daycare or outpatient care, and children live at home.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">08</span><span class="qa__q">Can my child go to school during cancer treatment?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Many children can go back to school during the less intensive phases of treatment, when blood counts are safe. Your doctor will advise when it is safe.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">09</span><span class="qa__q">What are the side effects of chemotherapy in children?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Common side effects are hair loss, nausea, mouth sores, tiredness and a higher risk of infections. Most go away after treatment, and the care team helps manage them.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">10</span><span class="qa__q">Will treatment affect my child’s growth or future fertility?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Some treatments can affect growth, hormones or fertility. The team plans treatment to reduce these risks, discusses fertility preservation for teenagers, and checks for late effects during follow-up.</p></details></div>
